[Music]  a tennessee man is building a brighter  and more connected community one  step at a time sean dromgool is a  long-time resident of his nashville  neighborhood known as 12  south after the death of george floyd he  posted to social media saying he was  afraid to walk alone because of the  color of his skin  our jamie wack shows us his  neighborhood's moving response  sean drumgool is no stranger to the view  outside his nashville home  the 30 year old has lived in the  neighborhood his entire life  his family for generations growing up it  was a mostly black community  everyone was family and then it changed  as the area grew in popularity sean  watched as his neighborhood became  gentrified  the black families he once played with  moved out and white families moved in  and a few years ago a pivotal turning  point sean says police stopped him  without reason and followed him home  to make sure he lived there  in a time when america is experiencing  nationwide protests over racial  inequality  he didn't feel safe to walk alone down  his street  what was it that made you feel fear  in a neighborhood that your family had  been a part of for 54 years  people saying um it's a suspicious  looking black man  walking down the street if you say  suspicious looking black man the police  come  ready and i'm dead before  i can even think it's a very real fear  as an african-american male  in 2020 as george floyd's story became  embedded in the american conscience  sean posted this i wanted to walk around  my neighborhood but the fear of not  returning home to my family alive  kept me on my front porch 50 people  responded oh my god i'm so sorry oh my  god i'll walk with you and there were 75  people in the parking lot it's like  wow i don't feel invisible i feel like  people care  i will never know what it's like to be  in the shoes of somebody  who has a different color skin than me  carrie conley was one of the neighbors  at the walk  i've lived here 15 years he's literally  like  three and a half blocks from me and i've  never seen him or his family  so it was a big wake-up call for me i  want to show you  the amazing neighbors that are here sean  began hosting  weekly neighborhood walks with hundreds  of strangers coming out to support him  turning it into a movement called we  walk with sean  the goal is simple get to know your  neighbors  when you're walking you're bound to have  conversations and when you have  conversations  you change you learn so that you can be  a positive  effect yeah it was that bridge that's  all that it was for carrie and sean  these one one-time strangers are now  like family  he calls me a sister it seems like i've  known him  my whole life if we don't stop to talk  to our neighbors we're missing out on  on so much we are only getting  started sean hopes he can bring the  movement to many u.s cities  and walk in other gentrified  neighborhoods this is  what happens to black and brown families  who remain in inner city  or suburban neighborhoods it's a bad  feeling  when you feel like you're a prisoner i  was literally felt like i was a prisoner  on my front porch you are sitting on the  steps  that you sat on and were afraid to move  from  how does it feel now i'm now free to  walk around the neighborhood  the whole world is behind me i am  getting to see  america live up to its creed of being a  more  perfect union i'm a part of a story that  begins  on the slave ships and now i get to see  this little part  of the world and become a better place  for cbs this morning jamie wax new york  it's great yeah watching those long  lines of people as i get the walks get  longer every day  and it really puts it in perspective  when you know the end there  you know descendants of slaves and now  here you are walking with  white people black people all kinds of  people and i think that  that walk should be happening more  throughout all neighborhoods in this  country  uh we do have some exciting news by the  way to share about jamie wax who just  did that story  we told you on saturday we're going to  tell you again now we can talk about  this every day because we're so happy  for jamie  he and his wife jacqueline welcomed a  baby girl there she is adelaide simone  wax was born july 30th was talking with  jamie over the weekend  um he sent me a text the day before yeah  going into labor at 10 o'clock i'm like  prayers up good luck could you imagine  all that  happening yeah such a great job at once  congrats man  congrats

